<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Mogaung</h1><p class="st-meta">1215 CE – 1796 CE (approx.) · capital Mogaung</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful Shan state in Kachin country of northern Myanmar, a centre of the jade trade that fought Ava and China before Konbaung absorbed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Mogaung, Shan Mong Kawng, on the Mogaung river in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachin-state">Kachin State</a>, was one of the northern Shan states; Shan chronicles date the founding of its ruling house to the early 13th century. It belonged to the wider world of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-mao">Tai Mao</a>, often linked to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mong-mao">Mong Mao</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its saopha ruled over Shan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachin-people">Kachin</a> and other upland peoples, drawing wealth from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> valleys and from the jade mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hpakant">Hpakant</a>, which supplied Chinese markets. Mogaung frequently raided the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ava-kingdom">Ava Kingdom</a>, joining other Shan states, and at times also paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming China</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a> subdued Mogaung in the 1550s, and its rebellions were crushed later. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty</a> abolished its saopha line in the late 18th century, placing the area under direct royal governors. The town of Mogaung remains in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachin-state">Kachin State</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mogaung.jpg" alt="Mogaung" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Saimawnkham · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moengkaung.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1215 CE – 1796 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mogaung</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Myanmar</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mong Kawng, Mogaung State</dd></dl></aside></div>
